# EZiL OS — the ONE place a shipped container image reference is pinned.
#
# Plain `KEY=value`, no quoting, no substitution, no `export`. It is read by
# `local/src/container/run-spec.ts` (`parseImagesEnv`) at RUNTIME on a user's
# machine, and by CI when it builds and pushes. Anything that needs an image
# reference reads it from here; nothing composes one from parts of its own.
#
# ── Tag scheme: {<git-sha>,<semver>,latest} ─────────────────────────────────
# Every push publishes the SAME image manifest under up to three tags:
#
#   <git-sha>   the 8-hex-character short SHA of the commit that built it.
#               IMMUTABLE and the only tag anything is ever pinned to here.
#               A running container keeps its image until it stops, so a
#               floating tag would mean two users on "the same version" are
#               running different bytes and nobody can say which.
#   <semver>    e.g. `0.3.1`, published on a release tag. Human-facing, still
#               immutable once pushed; convenience for a download page.
#   latest      moves on every push to the default branch. NEVER pinned here.
#               It exists so `docker pull …:latest` gets a person started, and
#               for nothing else.
#
# So: the *_TAG values below are always a git SHA or a semver. A `latest` in
# this file is a bug.
#
# ── The two images ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EZIL_DESKTOP_IMAGE / _TAG — the desktop sandbox image built from
#   `worker/Dockerfile`. This is what local mode actually runs.
#
# EZIL_NEKO_IMAGE / _TAG / _OVERLAY_TAG — the neko + code-server base and its
#   EZiL-branded overlay. `worker/Dockerfile`'s first stage
#   (`FROM ${NEKO_IMAGE} AS neko`) FROMs the OVERLAY, not the bare base — see
#   the pairing rule below.
#
#   EZIL_NEKO_TAG is the BARE base (both public upstream SHAs, no EZiL
#   branding): `docker/neko/build.sh` derives it from `docker/neko/pins.env`'s
#   `NEKO_SHA`/`NEKO_APPS_SHA` (first 8 hex chars of each, joined by `-`) and
#   `.github/workflows/image.yml` pushes it to `EZIL_NEKO_IMAGE:EZIL_NEKO_TAG`.
#
#   EZIL_NEKO_OVERLAY_TAG is `worker/assets/neko-branding/Dockerfile` built on
#   top of that exact base: brand2 adds `/var/www/ezil-mobile.js` (the mobile
#   on-screen keyboard); brand3 adds the black-picture detector to it — "the
#   only code in the product that can read the decoded picture" (that
#   Dockerfile's own header). `worker/Dockerfile`'s `ARG NEKO_IMAGE` FROMs
#   `EZIL_NEKO_IMAGE:EZIL_NEKO_OVERLAY_TAG`, never the bare tag — wiring the
#   bare `EZIL_NEKO_TAG` straight into that ARG would silently ship a desktop
#   with no mobile keyboard and no black-screen detection.
#
#   🔴 PAIRING RULE — the three lines below, plus `worker/Dockerfile:77`'s own
#   `ARG NEKO_IMAGE=`, encode the SAME two upstream SHAs and MUST be bumped
#   together in the same commit as a new overlay build:
#     1. `EZIL_NEKO_TAG` here = `<neko-sha8>-<neko-apps-sha8>` (from
#        `docker/neko/pins.env`, per that file's own pairing-rule comment).
#     2. `EZIL_NEKO_OVERLAY_TAG` here = `EZIL_NEKO_TAG` + `-ezil-brandN` (N is
#        whichever `worker/assets/neko-branding/Dockerfile` content is
#        currently built and published — bump N only when `worker/assets/
#        neko-branding/**` actually changes, per that Dockerfile's own header:
#        "THE OVERLAY SUFFIX MUST BE BUMPED EVERY TIME THE CONTENT OF www/
#        CHANGES").
#     3. `worker/Dockerfile`'s `ARG NEKO_IMAGE` = `EZIL_NEKO_IMAGE:` + #2.
#   `.github/workflows/image.yml`'s "Load image pins" step enforces #1 = the
#   pins.env-derived value, #3 = `EZIL_NEKO_IMAGE:` + #2, AND that #2 has #1 as
#   its prefix (`<EZIL_NEKO_TAG>-<suffix>`) — a suffix bumped without a base
#   bump, or vice versa, fails the build rather than silently drifting.

EZIL_DESKTOP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ezilhq/ezil-os-desktop
# 🔴 PLACEHOLDER, ON PURPOSE, AND IT IS NOT A VALID DOCKER TAG.
# Nothing has been pushed to GHCR yet (no tags exist in this repository, so
# `deploy.yml` has never run). `run-spec.ts` validates every tag it reads
# against Docker's tag grammar and REFUSES to compose a reference from one
# that fails, falling back to `LOCAL_DESKTOP_IMAGE_FALLBACK` — the image the
# `worker/Dockerfile` build actually produces on this machine — and saying so.
# A placeholder that looks like configuration must fail at read time, not at
# `docker run` time. CI replaces this with the build's short SHA.
#
# 🔴 HAND-OFF: `.github/workflows/image.yml`'s `desktop` job runs with
# `permissions: contents: read` on purpose (a workflow committing to `main`
# needs `contents: write` and races the branch ruleset), so it CANNOT write
# the tag it just published back into this file. Instead it publishes the
# published tag + digest two ways: the run's job summary (human-readable),
# and an uploaded `actions/upload-artifact@v4` artifact named
# `published-images.env` containing a ready-to-paste `EZIL_DESKTOP_TAG=<sha8>`
# line. A human (or row R2's release step) advances this pin from that
# artifact:
#   gh run download <run-id> -R EZiLHQ/ezil-os -n published-images.env
#   # then paste its EZIL_DESKTOP_TAG=<sha8> line over the one below, commit.
# Until that happens this placeholder stays exactly as shipped — it already
# fails `isDockerTag`'s grammar check (`run-spec.ts`), so `resolveDesktopImage`
# already falls back to `LOCAL_DESKTOP_IMAGE_FALLBACK` with a named reason
# instead of composing a broken reference. No value change was needed here.
EZIL_DESKTOP_TAG=<to be pinned by CI>

EZIL_NEKO_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ezilhq/ezil-neko-vscode
EZIL_NEKO_TAG=d74052bb-049931d7
EZIL_NEKO_OVERLAY_TAG=d74052bb-049931d7-ezil-brand8
